# WorldPartition coverage and lifecycle tasks

Source: [approved proposal](WorldPartition.CoverageAndLifecycle.Proposal.md). Execute in order; the proposal remains the detailed contract. All grids are anchored at world zero. Preserve consumer-owned lighting algorithms and existing worker/GPU infrastructure.

## 1. Shared partition foundation

Outcome: independent partition instances can request and safely publish spatial cells.

- [x] Add registration, world scope, instance/cell identity, configurable cell extents, floor-based coordinates, and source targeting; replace kind-only identity and half-block center assumptions.
- [x] Implement required/prefetch/retained coverage, intersecting-cell selection, multi-source union, incremental updates, hysteresis, and explicit capacity shortfalls.
- [x] Centralize acknowledged lifecycle transitions, revisions/incarnations, cancellation, retries, publication validation, and world/registration teardown; keep residency separate from content readiness.
- [x] Enforce owning-thread transitions, immutable work/completions, render-thread publication, and fair shared/per-partition residency, in-flight, capture, dispatch, and upload limits.
- [x] Verify with reusable providers: negative/fractional/large coordinates, multiple instances/sources, boundary oscillation, teleports, delayed/stale completions, dirtying, slot reuse, missing dependencies, teardown, and starvation. Gate: required coverage is correct and obsolete work cannot publish.

Completion evidence: [foundation review and validation](WorldPartition.Foundation.Evidence.md). Independent completion audit passed; 62 tests passed (43 foundation, 19 existing regressions).

## 2. Near-field geometry partition

Outcome: near-tracing coverage follows its own requirements instead of the occupancy scene's window.

- [x] Register independent 16-block geometry cells in the approved fixed 3-by-3-by-3 window. Exclude out-of-world vertical demand, retain no extra prefetch/retention fringe, and leave incomplete ray segments explicitly unresolved.
- [x] Reuse chunk snapshots and `NearFieldCellCapture`; coalesce 32-block source reads and invalidate dependent subcells on source changes. Keep missing chunks distinct from unsupported geometry.
- [x] Generalize `NearFieldGpuScene`, UBOs, shaders, and readiness addressing together; retain overlapping ring cells and coherent geometry/light/material publication. Prefetch must complete GPU residency before activation.
- [x] Transfer near-field window/publication ownership out of the occupancy renderer, preserving publication revisions and history invalidation. Gate: one lifecycle owner, no stale slot exposure, and existing near-field tests pass.

Current completion evidence: [near-field geometry, publication and final validation](WorldPartition.CoverageDiagnostics.Evidence.md#final-completion-review). This current record supersedes the former missing item 2 evidence link and includes the fixed-window corrections and acceptance verified under item 3.

## 3. Coverage evidence and diagnostics

Outcome: ready coverage and resource limits are observable and validated.

- [x] Extend World Cell Bounds and Near-Field Geometry for partition/source bounds, desired versus ready coverage, and distinct unpublished/unsupported/out-of-coverage states; expose per-partition backlog, retries, stale completions, and capacity metrics.
- [x] Sweep every position through a publication-cell interval; test immediate desired versus eventual ready coverage with delayed providers, movement, teleports, dirty updates, negative/large coordinates, and camera motion.
- [x] Compare capture duplication, movement upload bytes, frame-thread work, resident memory, and readiness latency; validate the fixed 48-block window and select scheduling budgets from those measurements. Gate: record separate unit/GPU and live-runtime evidence without claiming instantaneous readiness under overload.

Completion evidence: [coverage diagnostics and final review](WorldPartition.CoverageDiagnostics.Evidence.md#final-completion-review). All 178 focused tests passed, including 80 GPU cases. Runtime-budget tests verify bounded recovery after movement, teleports and edits; the user confirmed live alignment and lag resolution. Fixed 48-block coverage and the existing scheduling budgets are accepted. No additional manual walkthrough is required for this item.

## 4. Existing scene residency migration

Outcome: scene consumers share the coordinator without duplicate lifecycle authority.

- [x] Adapt `TraceSceneRegionScheduler` and `LumonSceneRegionScheduler`, preserving coverage/priority behavior and domain capture/relight queues; verify each adapter before removing replaced registry, coverage, and transition code.
- [x] Move reusable contracts/coordinator from `LumOn/WorldCells` into top-level `WorldPartition`; keep `WorldPartitionModSystem` thin and files responsibility-specific. Gate: scene regressions pass and each partition has exactly one residency owner.

Completion evidence: [scene residency migration and validation](WorldPartition.SceneResidency.Evidence.md). Second review and independent completion audit passed. 341 unique selected cases passed, including 115 GPU cases; the production build is clean. Two unchanged pre-existing shader source assertions are documented separately, and two async GPU cases passed in fresh test hosts.

## 5. World-probe evaluation and final acceptance

Outcome: evaluation complete; world probes intentionally retain their existing scheduler outside WorldPartition, with near-field geometry independently complete.

- [x] Evaluate one partition per clipmap level against fixed world-zero boundaries, current probe lattice/ring behavior, stale-work rejection, and per-frame cost. Migrate only if equivalence and removal of duplicate residency code are demonstrated; otherwise document deferral.
- [x] Preserve importance selection, directional budgets, lighting confidence/history, atlas packing, and the distinction between residency and valid lighting.
- [x] Run required builds/regressions through a validation subagent; update architecture/invariants and evidence records. Gate: all mandatory proposal requirements are verified, temporary adapters are removed where replaced, and any conditional probe deferral is documented.

Completion evidence: [world-probe evaluation and final acceptance](WorldPartition.WorldProbeEvaluation.Evidence.md). The user decided not to migrate world probes after reviewing the evaluation; the fixed-zero mapping is possible, but lifetime/publication equivalence and a justified removal/cost benefit are not demonstrated. Second review and independent final audit passed. 486 unique selected cases passed, including 152 GPU cases; production build is clean. The evidence records exclusions and a separate pre-existing late probe completion defect.

Coverage decision: near-field uses a fixed 48-block window with no additional prefetch or retention. Item 3 validates and retains the current scheduling budgets. Items 1 through 5 are complete, with world-probe migration explicitly excluded.
